<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Catalan Republic (1641)</h1><p class="st-meta">1641 CE – 1641 CE · capital Barcelona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Catalan republic proclaimed during the Reapers' War, which lasted barely a week before Catalonia placed itself under the French king.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a> rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV</a> in 1640 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reapers-war">Reapers' War</a>, provoked by the billeting of royal troops and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares">Count-Duke of Olivares's</a> demands. As a royal army advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pau-claris">Pau Claris</a>, president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalitat-de-catalunya">Generalitat</a>, proclaimed a Catalan Republic under French protection in January 1641.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was an emergency regime run by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalitat-de-catalunya">Generalitat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona's</a> councils, preoccupied with defence. Unable to resist Castilian forces alone, its leaders quickly decided that French support required a monarch.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 23 January 1641 the Catalan authorities recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII of France</a> as Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, ending the republic. Days later Catalan and French troops defeated the royal army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montjuic-1641">Montjuïc</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pau-claris">Claris</a> died in February, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV</a>'s rule in 1652, losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1659.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1641 CE – 1641 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.36%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1641 CE – 1641 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Barcelona</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pau Claris</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Catalan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Catalan Republic of Pau Claris</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1641&amp;focus=catalan-republic-1641" aria-label="Catalan Republic (1641) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4733 702 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Catalan Republic (1641) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5053 958L5053 958M5053 958L5054 728M5053 958L4909 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5053 958h0M5054 728h0M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5053 958h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5053 958h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Barcelona. <a href="/atlas?year=1641&amp;focus=catalan-republic-1641">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
